  <main class="lesson-main">
    <div class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="../../../../../entrance-exam/index.html">Entrance Exam</a> ›
      <a href="../../index.html">Grade 12</a> ›
      <a href="../index.html">Mathematics</a> ›
      Unit 4 · Lesson 1
    </div>

    <div class="lesson-header">
      <div class="lesson-unit-tag">Unit 4 · Integral Calculus</div>
      <h1>∫ Antiderivatives and Indefinite Integrals</h1>
      <p>Discover integration — the reverse of differentiation. Learn to find antiderivatives, apply all basic integration rules, use substitution for composite functions, and master the constant of integration C.</p>
      <div class="lesson-meta">
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">📖 <strong>Reading:</strong> ~25 min</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">✏️ <strong>Examples:</strong> 5 worked</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">🎯 <strong>Quiz:</strong> 5 questions</div>
        <div class="lesson-meta-item">🎥 <strong>Videos:</strong> 4 resources</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="section-block" id="intro">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">🎯</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Introduction</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">Going backwards from the derivative</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="content-box">
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          You have spent Units 2 and 3 learning differentiation — starting with a function and finding its derivative. Now we reverse the process. Given a <strong>derivative</strong>, can we find the <strong>original function</strong>?
        </p>
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          For example, if f'(x) = 2x, what is f(x)? You might guess f(x) = x². But f(x) = x² + 5 also works, and so does f(x) = x² − 100. In fact, any function of the form <strong>f(x) = x² + C</strong> works. This is the essence of <strong>indefinite integration</strong> — finding a family of functions that differ only by a constant.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--text)">By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
        <ul style="margin-top:.75rem;padding-left:1.25rem;font-size:.9rem;line-height:2;color:var(--text)">
          <li>Define an antiderivative and explain why +C is always included</li>
          <li>Use the notation ∫f(x)dx correctly</li>
          <li>Apply the Power Rule for integration: ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C</li>
          <li>Integrate sums, differences and scalar multiples</li>
          <li>Integrate basic trigonometric, exponential and logarithmic functions</li>
          <li>Use substitution (u-substitution) for composite functions</li>
          <li>Find a specific antiderivative given an initial condition</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- EXPLANATION -->
    <div class="section-block" id="explanation">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">📖</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Explanation</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">Complete theory with visual aids</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Differentiation vs Integration visual -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.1 Differentiation vs Integration — Two Directions</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          Differentiation and integration are inverse operations — like multiplication and division:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-int-visual">
          <div class="di-block di-f">
            f(x)<br>
            <span style="font-size:.7rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7)">original function</span>
          </div>
          <div class="di-arrow-col">
            <div class="di-top">d/dx → differentiate</div>
            <div style="text-align:center;font-size:.75rem;color:var(--text-muted);padding:.25rem">↕ reverse operations</div>
            <div class="di-bottom">← ∫dx integrate</div>
          </div>
          <div class="di-block di-fprime">
            f'(x)<br>
            <span style="font-size:.7rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7)">derivative</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 Definition — Antiderivative</div>
          <p>F(x) is an <strong>antiderivative</strong> of f(x) if <strong>F'(x) = f(x)</strong>.<br><br>
          The <strong>indefinite integral</strong> of f(x) is the family of all antiderivatives:<br><br>
          <strong>∫f(x) dx = F(x) + C</strong><br><br>
          where C is an arbitrary constant called the <strong>constant of integration</strong>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Why C matters visual -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.2 Why the Constant C is Essential</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem">
          All of these functions have derivative 2x. They are all correct antiderivatives of 2x:
        </p>
        <div class="visual-box">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 480 200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <rect x="0" y="0" width="480" height="200" fill="#F8FAFC" rx="8"/>
            <!-- Axes -->
            <line x1="240" y1="10" x2="240" y2="190" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="20" y1="130" x2="460" y2="130" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- x²+5 (top) -->
            <path d="M100,10 Q240,30 380,10" stroke="#14532D" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/>
            <text x="390" y="14" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="11" fill="#14532D" font-weight="700">x²+5</text>
            <!-- x² (middle) -->
            <path d="M100,60 Q240,80 380,60" stroke="#2563EB" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/>
            <text x="390" y="65" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="11" fill="#2563EB" font-weight="700">x²</text>
            <!-- x²-3 (below) -->
            <path d="M100,100 Q240,120 380,100" stroke="#DC2626" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/>
            <text x="390" y="104" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="11" fill="#DC2626" font-weight="700">x²−3</text>
            <!-- x²-8 (bottom) -->
            <path d="M100,148 Q240,168 380,148" stroke="#7C3AED" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/>
            <text x="390" y="152" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="11" fill="#7C3AED" font-weight="700">x²−8</text>
            <!-- Label -->
            <text x="240" y="188" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#64748B" text-anchor="middle">All have slope (derivative) = 2x at every point</text>
            <!-- Bracket -->
            <rect x="10" y="8" width="80" height="165" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="#22C55E" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,2"/>
            <text x="50" y="188" font-family="Arial" font-size="9" fill="#166534" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Family: x²+C</text>
          </svg>
          <div class="visual-caption">All parabolas in the family x²+C have the same shape and the same derivative 2x. The constant C shifts them vertically but does not change the slope.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box">
          <div class="note-label">⚠ Never Forget +C</div>
          <p>On the entrance exam, leaving out +C in an indefinite integral is an error. The answer ∫2x dx = x² is <strong>incomplete</strong>. The correct answer is ∫2x dx = x² + C. Always include it unless you have an initial condition that lets you find the value of C.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Integration notation -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.3 Integration Notation Explained</h3>
        <div class="visual-box">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 480 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <rect x="0" y="0" width="480" height="120" fill="#F8FAFC" rx="8"/>
            <!-- Integral symbol and parts -->
            <text x="60" y="75" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="50" fill="#14532D" font-weight="700">∫</text>
            <text x="95" y="72" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="28" fill="#0F172A" font-weight="700">f(x)</text>
            <text x="155" y="72" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="28" fill="#7C3AED" font-weight="700">dx</text>
            <text x="195" y="72" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="22" fill="#64748B">=</text>
            <text x="218" y="72" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="28" fill="#166534" font-weight="700">F(x) + C</text>
            <!-- Labels with arrows -->
            <line x1="62" y1="90" x2="62" y2="108" stroke="#14532D" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="62" y="118" font-family="Arial" font-size="9" fill="#14532D" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">integral sign</text>
            <line x1="115" y1="90" x2="115" y2="108" stroke="#0F172A" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="115" y="118" font-family="Arial" font-size="9" fill="#0F172A" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">integrand</text>
            <line x1="163" y1="90" x2="163" y2="108" stroke="#7C3AED" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="163" y="118" font-family="Arial" font-size="9" fill="#7C3AED" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">w.r.t x</text>
            <line x1="310" y1="90" x2="310" y2="108" stroke="#166534" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="310" y="118" font-family="Arial" font-size="9" fill="#166534" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">antiderivative + constant</text>
          </svg>
          <div class="visual-caption">∫ is the integral sign. f(x) is the integrand (what you integrate). dx tells you the variable. F(x)+C is the result.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Basic integration rules -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.4 Basic Integration Rules</h3>
        <table class="rules-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Function f(x)</th>
              <th>Integral ∫f(x)dx</th>
              <th>Rule Name</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">k (constant)</td>
              <td class="anti">kx + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Constant Rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">xⁿ (n ≠ −1)</td>
              <td class="anti">xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Power Rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">1/x = x⁻¹</td>
              <td class="anti">ln|x| + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Log Rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">eˣ</td>
              <td class="anti">eˣ + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Exponential Rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">aˣ</td>
              <td class="anti">aˣ/ln(a) + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">General Exponential</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">sin(x)</td>
              <td class="anti">−cos(x) + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Trig Rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">cos(x)</td>
              <td class="anti">sin(x) + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Trig Rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="fn">sec²(x)</td>
              <td class="anti">tan(x) + C</td>
              <td style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--text-muted)">Trig Rule</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="note-box">
          <div class="note-label">⚠ Power Rule Exception</div>
          <p>The power rule ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C does NOT work when n = −1. That case is handled separately: ∫x⁻¹ dx = ∫(1/x) dx = ln|x| + C.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- U-substitution -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.5 U-Substitution (Reverse Chain Rule)</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:.75rem">
          When the integrand is a composite function, use substitution to simplify it. This is the integration version of the chain rule.
        </p>
        <div class="definition-box">
          <div class="def-label">📌 U-Substitution Method</div>
          <p>
            1. Let <strong>u = g(x)</strong> (the inner function)<br>
            2. Find <strong>du = g'(x) dx</strong><br>
            3. Rewrite the integral entirely in terms of u<br>
            4. Integrate with respect to u<br>
            5. Substitute back: replace u with g(x)<br><br>
            Example: ∫2x(x²+1)⁵ dx<br>
            Let u = x²+1, then du = 2x dx<br>
            = ∫u⁵ du = u⁶/6 + C = (x²+1)⁶/6 + C
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Initial conditions -->
      <div class="content-box">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--heading);margin-bottom:.875rem">2.6 Finding C with Initial Conditions</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:.75rem">
          When a specific function value is given (initial condition), you can find the exact value of C:
        </p>
        <div class="math-display">
          Step 1: Integrate to get F(x) + C &nbsp;|&nbsp;
          Step 2: Substitute the given point &nbsp;|&nbsp;
          Step 3: Solve for C
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--text-muted)">
          Example: Find f(x) if f'(x) = 3x² and f(1) = 5.<br>
          f(x) = ∫3x² dx = x³ + C. Using f(1) = 5: 1 + C = 5 → C = 4. So f(x) = x³ + 4.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- FORMULA REFERENCE -->
    <div class="section-block" id="formula">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">📋</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Formula Reference Card</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">All integration rules — memorize these</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card">
        <div class="formula-card-title">📐 Unit 4 Lesson 1 — Indefinite Integrals</div>
        <div class="formula-grid">
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Power Rule</div>
            <div class="formula-math">∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Valid for n ≠ −1. Add 1 to exponent, divide by new exponent.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Constant Rule</div>
            <div class="formula-math">∫k dx = kx + C</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Integrate a constant by multiplying by x</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Log Rule</div>
            <div class="formula-math">∫(1/x) dx = ln|x| + C</div>
            <div class="formula-note">The exception to the power rule (n = −1)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Exponential</div>
            <div class="formula-math">∫eˣ dx = eˣ + C</div>
            <div class="formula-note">eˣ is its own integral (and derivative)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">Sin and Cos</div>
            <div class="formula-math">∫sin x dx = −cos x + C</div>
            <div class="formula-note">∫cos x dx = sin x + C</div>
          </div>
          <div class="formula-item">
            <div class="formula-name">U-Substitution</div>
            <div class="formula-math">∫f(g(x))g'(x)dx = F(g(x))+C</div>
            <div class="formula-note">Let u=g(x), du=g'(x)dx, then integrate</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- WORKED EXAMPLES -->
    <div class="section-block" id="examples">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-icon">✏️</div>
        <div>
          <div class="section-title-text">Worked Examples</div>
          <div class="section-subtitle">5 exam-style problems solved step by step</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 1</span>
          <span class="example-title">Basic Power Rule Integration</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Find ∫(4x³ − 6x² + 5x − 3) dx</div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Apply</span>
            <div class="step-text">Integrate term by term using power rule:<br>
              <span class="math">∫4x³ dx = 4·(x⁴/4) = x⁴</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫−6x² dx = −6·(x³/3) = −2x³</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫5x dx = 5·(x²/2) = 5x²/2</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫−3 dx = −3x</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ ∫(4x³−6x²+5x−3) dx = <strong>x⁴ − 2x³ + 5x²/2 − 3x + C</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 2</span>
          <span class="example-title">Integrating with Negative and Fractional Exponents</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Find ∫(√x + 1/x² + 3) dx</div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Rewrite</span>
            <div class="step-text">Convert to power notation:<br>
              <span class="math">√x = x^(1/2), &nbsp; 1/x² = x⁻²</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Integrate</span>
            <div class="step-text">
              <span class="math">∫x^(1/2) dx = x^(3/2)/(3/2) = (2/3)x^(3/2)</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫x⁻² dx = x⁻¹/(−1) = −1/x</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫3 dx = 3x</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ = <strong>(2/3)x^(3/2) − 1/x + 3x + C</strong> &nbsp; or &nbsp; <strong>(2/3)√x³ − 1/x + 3x + C</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 3</span>
          <span class="example-title">U-Substitution</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Find ∫6x(x²+4)³ dx</div>
          <div class="example-step"><span class="step-badge">Let u</span><div class="step-text"><span class="math">u = x²+4 &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; du = 2x dx &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; 6x dx = 3 du</span></div></div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Substitute</span>
            <div class="step-text"><span class="math">∫6x(x²+4)³ dx = ∫u³ · 3 du = 3∫u³ du = 3·(u⁴/4) = 3u⁴/4</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Back-substitute</span>
            <div class="step-text"><span class="math">= 3(x²+4)⁴/4 + C</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ ∫6x(x²+4)³ dx = <strong>3(x²+4)⁴/4 + C</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 4</span>
          <span class="example-title">Trigonometric Integral</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> Find ∫(3cos x − 2sin x + 4eˣ) dx</div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Apply rules</span>
            <div class="step-text">
              <span class="math">∫3cos x dx = 3sin x</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫−2sin x dx = 2cos x</span><br>
              <span class="math">∫4eˣ dx = 4eˣ</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ = <strong>3sin x + 2cos x + 4eˣ + C</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example-card">
        <div class="example-header">
          <span class="example-num">Example 5</span>
          <span class="example-title">Finding C with an Initial Condition</span>
        </div>
        <div class="example-body">
          <div class="example-problem"><strong>Problem:</strong> The gradient of a curve is dy/dx = 6x² − 4x + 1. The curve passes through (1, 3). Find the equation of the curve.</div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Integrate</span>
            <div class="step-text"><span class="math">y = ∫(6x²−4x+1) dx = 2x³−2x²+x+C</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-step">
            <span class="step-badge">Use (1,3)</span>
            <div class="step-text"><span class="math">3 = 2(1)³−2(1)²+(1)+C = 2−2+1+C = 1+C &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; C = 2</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="example-answer">✅ Equation of curve: <strong>y = 2x³ − 2x² + x + 2</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

